built.notes:
Files: design/flow/screens/game-classic-night.html, design/flow/screens/result-classic-night.html. Shots: design/flow/shots/game-classic-night-1.png, -2.png, result-classic-night-1.png, plus the rejected lit-pass comparison game-classic-night-litpass-alt-1.png and -2.png. game-classic.html was not touched. The 'Truthy · BOARDING PASS' carrier band wording is kept on both screens, in line with the user's 'boarding pass deyim' request.

Dark pass or lit pass: I tried both and kept the DARK pass (slate #252f41 on a #0b1528 to #1c2538 sky). Why:
(1) A lit cream pass is a bright slab covering about 60% of the screen. In a dark room it glares, which defeats the point of a night theme. The dark pass reads as a dim object lit from within and is clearly the calmer of the two.
(2) The dark pass is a true token remap. The lit pass also needed scoped overrides on non-root rules, because with dark ink the flight path, path labels, focus ring and the ink-filled Next button vanished into the night sky (.route needed its own --ink and --surface-raised, and .btn.next needed new colours).
(3) On dark paper, the light True/False/correct colours used for text on the pass and for filled buttons can be one token each. Their button text (--on-dark) becomes dark navy.

Trade-offs to know about:
- The Next and Play again pill, which is --ink, becomes a light warm pill. It is the most luminous surface in the answer state, but it is a neutral, not an accent, and it is the one action.
- The barcode turns into light bars. That is acceptable but a touch busier than in day mode.

Only change outside :root: the hard-coded shadow rgba values moved into --shadow-sm, --shadow-btn and --shadow-press, and a new --on-accent token now carries the dark ink on the amber band and plane (in day mode it would equal var(--ink)).

Complete night :root block (identical in both files):
:root {
  --sky-1: #0b1528;
  --sky-2: #101c33;
  --sky-3: #15233d;
  --sky-4: #1c2538;
  --surface: var(--sky-2);
  --surface-raised: #252f41;
  --surface-sunk: #1d2636;
  --ink: #e6e3da;
  --ink-muted: #9ba4b3;
  --rule: #3c4759;
  --accent: #e0a52c;
  --true: #6f9ae0;
  --false: #e2836f;
  --correct: #55b88e;
  --wrong: #e2836f;
  --on-dark: #0f1a2c;
  --on-accent: #141c2b; /* NEW: ink on the amber band and the plane; day value var(--ink) */
  --cloud: #2c3a55;
  --r: 16px; --r-sm: 8px;
  --shadow: 0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.2), 0 22px 36px -18px rgba(0,0,0,.75);
  --shadow-sm: 0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.2), 0 6px 12px -6px rgba(0,0,0,.6); /* NEW: was hard-coded on .close */
  --shadow-btn: 0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.2), 0 10px 18px -10px rgba(0,0,0,.7); /* NEW: was hard-coded on .btn */
  --shadow-press: 0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.2); /* NEW: was hard-coded on .btn:active */
  --t1: 120ms; --t2: 220ms; --t3: 360ms;
  --spring: cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1);
  --ease: cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1);
  --sans: 'Overpass', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --mono: 'Overpass Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
  --notch: 12px;
}

Day equivalents for the new tokens, so the day :root can adopt them with no visual change:
--on-accent: var(--ink);
--shadow-sm: 0 1px 0 rgba(16,35,63,.1), 0 6px 12px -6px rgba(16,35,63,.4);
--shadow-btn: 0 1px 0 rgba(16,35,63,.1), 0 10px 18px -10px rgba(16,35,63,.55);
--shadow-press: 0 1px 0 rgba(16,35,63,.1);

Measured contrast on the night ground (WCAG):
- ink on paper 10.48, ink on slip 11.83
- muted on paper 5.35
- true on paper 4.72, on slip 5.33
- false on paper 4.94, on slip 5.58
- correct on slip 6.24
- button text on true 6.12, on false 6.40, on ink 13.59
- on-accent on amber 7.79

Amber (luminance 0.44) stays brighter than true (0.32), false (0.34) and correct (0.38).
